{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses went and spoke these words.</b> All the commentators struggled with this going, for it is not specified where he went. And it appears to me to explain it in two ways. The first is this: because Moses wanted to say 'I am no longer able to go out and come in,' and the word 'I am not able' (לֹא אוּכַל) has two aspects: either 'I am not able' literally, for lack of physical ability, or 'I am not able' meaning 'I am not permitted,' as in the language of 'לֹא תוּכַל לֶאֱכֹל בִּשְׁעָרֶיךָ' (Deuteronomy 12:17). And indeed here its meaning is: I am no longer permitted to go out and come in, because permission has already been given to Joshua. Now Moses was afraid lest Israel understand 'I am not able' in its literal sense — lacking the physical ability to go, to come in and go out before them as time after time — so what did Moses do? He went before all Israel with alacrity, the length and breadth of the camp, for by this he showed them that his physical power then was as now, to walk and to go out and to come in before them, and then they would understand on their own the statement 'I am not able' — that his intention was 'I am not permitted.' Therefore it says 'And Moses went' — that he walked before all Israel with alacrity, to show them that he is able but not permitted. And for this reason he said to them: 'A hundred and twenty years old am I today,' and nevertheless my power then is as now, for in all these days my strength has not failed, and this is a proof that I am able but not permitted. And in this manner one may say 'And Moses went' also for the other matter that Rashi explained: 'I am not able to go out and come in' in matters of halakhah; therefore 'And Moses went' — that he walked before them in matters of halakhah and Torah, and by this he showed them that his power then is as now in matters of halakhah, and he is able but not permitted, for permission has been given to Joshua, and of necessity the wellsprings of wisdom were stopped up from him, and this stoppage is that he is not permitted as stated." "The second is this: that Moses wanted to spur them on to repentance, whose essence lies in words, as it is written (Hosea 14:3): 'קְחוּ עִמָּכֶם דְּבָרִים וְשׁוּבוּ אֶל ה׳' — 'Take with you words and return to HaShem.' Therefore it says 'And Moses went and spoke these words to all Israel,' and since it says immediately after: 'And he said to them, A hundred and twenty years old am I today' — if so, where did the first utterance go? Rather, it was a harsh utterance of rebuke, and therefore he spoke it to all Israel, to say: whoever has something to answer, let him come and answer, as Rashi explained concerning what is said: 'אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה אֶל כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל' (Deuteronomy 1:1). And so too here he said to all of them the matter of repentance that depends on words. And since no person ever sees his own obligation, and every sinner and rebel will never go of himself to the sage, the physician, to seek a remedy for his malady of the soul's sickness — and it would be enough if he listened when the sage comes to him and speaks to his heart to draw him toward the way of repentance — therefore it says 'And Moses went': that he went from tent to tent to every single one of Israel and spoke to his heart 'these words,' that is, the matters of repentance that depend on words. And this turns upon what is written above: 'כִּי קָרוֹב אֵלֶיךָ הַדָּבָר מְאֹד בְּפִיךָ' — that is, the matter of 'Take with you words.'" "And perhaps it is the matter of peace, which they expounded upon the verse 'Seek peace and pursue it' (Psalms 34:15) — for peace is not like the other commandments, for all the other commandments such as tsitsit and mezuzah and challah — if one comes to your hand you are obligated to perform it, but you are not obligated to go searching after it. But peace — you are obligated to search after it (Leviticus Rabbah 9:9). And one may give a reason for this: why is peace different from all the other commandments in the Torah? And it will come rightly if we say that this peace is to make peace between Israel and their Father in heaven by means of repentance. And therefore you need to search after it from two sides: first, from the side of the sinner, who will never lay the blame on himself and never sees his own obligation as you see his obligation — therefore you must go after him, for he will not come after you; second, from the side that you yourself need this peace, for all Israel are guarantors for one another. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 86a): Great is repentance, for even when an individual does it, forgiveness is granted to him and to the entire world, as it is said (Hosea 14:5): 'אֶרְפָּא מְשׁוּבָתָם כִּי שָׁב אַפִּי מִמֶּנּוּ' — 'I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely, for My anger is turned away from him.' If so, this peace is for your own benefit; it is fitting that you should pursue it." "And even if the peace is taken in its plain sense — between a man and his fellow — nevertheless there is a great need in it for repentance, so that those who return may be many, for throughout the days of the year the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts only the repentance of the many; and an individual — when? Only during the Ten Days of Repentance. Therefore it is said (Isaiah 57:19): 'שָׁלוֹם שָׁלוֹם לָרָחוֹק וְלַקָּרוֹב' — 'Peace, peace, to the far and to the near.' For one who is far and draws near needs these two peaces: both peace between him and his Elohim, and peace between him and his fellow, and then 'אָמַר ה׳ וּרְפָאתִיו' — 'says HaShem, and I will heal him.' And just as this peace that brings about repentance obligates every person to pursue it, so Moses did. This is what it says: 'And Moses went' — for he went after them and spurred them on to repentance and to the ways that bring about repentance, namely the two peaces that we have mentioned. And both were included in his saying: 'And he spoke these words' — both words of confession between a person and HaShem, and words of appeasement between a person and his fellow, for concerning both it is said: 'קְחוּ עִמָּכֶם דְּבָרִים וְשׁוּבוּ אֶל ה׳.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:27.037847096Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}